Give this build its own bundle identifier: bar.esko.Ghostty

This build is signed differently from the official one, so it must not
claim com.mitchellh.ghostty. Launch Services and privacy permissions
(TCC) key on the bundle identifier. With two apps under one identifier
and different signatures, Launch Services may open either one, and a
permission recorded for one fails the signature check for the other.

Everything named after the identifier moves with it:
- the app's PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER in all three configurations, and
  the dock tile plugin's, now nested under the app's as Apple's
  convention for embedded bundles has it (bar.esko.Ghostty.DockTilePlugin)
- the defaults suite the plugin reads the custom icon from (the app's
  own defaults domain)
- build_config.bundle_id, so the Application Support and cache folders
  and the unified-logging subsystem match what macOS shows for the app

The gettext domain stays com.mitchellh.ghostty: the .mo files are built
under that literal (build/GhosttyI18n.zig), and i18n.zig now names it
itself instead of borrowing the bundle identifier. Notification,
pasteboard and type names stay too; they are strings, not identity.
GTK uses build_config.bundle_id as its icon name; this build is macOS
only.

